declare extern type InputContext extends Instance with
    --- Whether this context and its actions take part in resolution at all. A disabled context consumes
    --- nothing, so a lower-priority one bound to the same key starts firing the tick this goes false --
    --- which is exactly what closing a menu should do.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- Resolution order, highest first. It orders FALLTHROUGH and nothing else -- in particular it does
    --- not decide which clock the context is dispatched on, which is `Rate` (ADR 0039). Ties are broken
    --- by a stable order the engine reproduces on every run (R10), so two contexts at the same priority
    --- never swap places between one run and the next.
    Priority: number
    --- Whether this context CONSUMES the inputs its bindings name, hiding them from every
    --- lower-priority context. A modal dialog sinks; a HUD does not. Sinking is per input and not per
    --- context: a sinking context that binds Escape hides Escape and leaves W alone.
    Sink: boolean
    --- Which clock this context is dispatched on (ADR 0039). `Simulation` by default, because that is
    --- the safe one: an action nobody thought about fires where determinism holds and where the input
    --- replay can see it. `Render` is for camera look and for UI, and an action read from a Render
    --- context is frame-rate-dependent by construction.
    Rate: EnumInputRate
end

declare extern type InputAction extends Instance with
    --- What this action produces, and therefore what `GetState` returns. It belongs to the action
    --- rather than to the binding, because one action may be driven by a key, a stick and an on-screen
    --- button at once and all three have to answer in the same currency.
    Type: EnumInputActionType
    --- Whether this action resolves. A disabled action consumes nothing even inside a sinking context,
    --- so disabling one hands its input back to whatever is underneath.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- Fired on the tick a `Bool` action becomes true. Never fires for any other action type: an axis
    --- has no moment of being pressed.
    read Pressed: Signal<()>
    --- Fired on the tick a `Bool` action becomes false, including when the action or its context is
    --- disabled while it was held -- a held key that stops being delivered has been released as far as
    --- the game is concerned, and leaving it stuck true is how a character keeps running after a menu
    --- opens.
    read Released: Signal<()>
    --- Fired on any tick this action's value changed, whatever its type. It carries NO arguments and
    --- the handler reads `GetState()` -- the same arrangement `Destroying` and
    --- `GetPropertyChangedSignal` use, and for the same reason (ADR 0039): the queue between the engine
    --- and the VM carries POD facts, an action's value is one of four types, and a signal whose
    --- argument had to be rebuilt at drain time would be a fire that captured nothing. Input dispatches
    --- once per tick, so the value read in the handler is the value that caused the fire.
    read StateChanged: Signal<()>
    --- This action's value as of the current tick -- a boolean for `Bool`, a number for `Direction1D`,
    --- a `Vector2` for `Direction2D` and `ViewportPosition`, and a `vector` for `Direction3D`. It is a
    --- SNAPSHOT: two calls inside one tick agree, and a recorded input stream hands the same answers
    --- back with no hardware attached, which is what makes the replay a replay of input rather than of
    --- a bot.
    function GetState(self): boolean | number | Vector2 | vector
    --- The binding a prompt should draw for this action -- "Press [E]". With a device type, the first
    --- child binding belonging to that family in document order; without one, the family of
    --- `InputService.LastInputDeviceType`, so a prompt follows whatever the player last touched. nil
    --- when the action has no binding for that device.
    function GetPreferredBinding(self, deviceType: EnumInputDeviceType?): InputBinding?
end

declare extern type InputBinding extends Instance with
    --- The input this binding names. `Unknown` means unbound, which is why the enum carries that item:
    --- an unset binding is expressible without the property being nullable, and the engine's value
    --- domain stays a closed set (ADR 0039).
    KeyCode: EnumKeyCode
    --- The composite key for +Y, on a `Direction1D` or `Direction2D` action. The four composites are
    --- how a keyboard drives an axis: W/A/S/D is one binding, not four.
    Up: EnumKeyCode
    --- The composite key for -Y.
    Down: EnumKeyCode
    --- The composite key for -X.
    Left: EnumKeyCode
    --- The composite key for +X.
    Right: EnumKeyCode
    --- What this binding's value is multiplied by before it reaches the action. Mouse look wants a
    --- small one and a stick wants 1; a negative one inverts an axis, which is what a settings screen's
    --- "invert Y" writes.
    Scale: number
    --- What a prompt shows for this binding. A localization key is allowed and is the intended use
    --- (§6): a binding that says "ui.prompt.interact" translates and one that says "Interact" does
    --- not. Empty means the engine has nothing to offer and the prompt should fall back to the key's
    --- own name. **The engine never acts on this and never will**, which is what makes it different
    --- from an `Inert` property: it is data a GAME reads back through `GetPreferredBinding` to draw its
    --- own prompt. There is no prompt widget in v1 and there is not meant to be one -- a rebinding
    --- screen is a game's screen.
    DisplayName: string
    --- A glyph for this binding, as an `asset://` URI. Empty means none -- a prompt with no glyph falls
    --- back to `DisplayName`, which is why the two are set together or not at all. **The engine never
    --- acts on this and never will**, for exactly the reason `DisplayName` gives: it is the same datum
    --- in a picture instead of a word, read back by a GAME through `GetPreferredBinding` to draw its
    --- own prompt. Nothing in the engine draws a prompt, so nothing in the engine loads this texture --
    --- an `ImageLabel` the game points at it does.
    Image: Content
    --- Which device family this binding belongs to, DERIVED from `KeyCode` (ADR 0039). It is read-only
    --- because a settable one could disagree with the key beside it -- `DeviceType = Gamepad` on a
    --- binding whose KeyCode is W is a statement the engine would then have to choose whether to
    --- believe. Anything that is not a gamepad input reports `KeyboardMouse`, `Unknown` included.
    read DeviceType: EnumInputDeviceType
end

declare extern type ScreenGui extends Instance with
    --- Whether this tree is laid out and drawn at all. A disabled ScreenGui costs one boolean read per
    --- frame; it does not cost a layout.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- Which ScreenGui draws on top when two overlap, highest last. `ZIndex` orders WITHIN a tree and
    --- this orders BETWEEN trees, so a HUD and a modal never have to agree on a shared numbering.
    DisplayOrder: number
    --- The height of the window this tree was laid out for, or 0 for pixels. With one set, every
    --- offset, text size, corner and stroke under it is in units of a window that tall, and the engine
    --- scales the whole tree to the window it has -- so a layout drawn for a 720-line monitor takes the
    --- same share of a phone with twice the lines, where in pixels it would be half its size. The width
    --- follows: the window is as many units wide as its shape gives that height, so anchor to edges
    --- with `Scale` and size with `Offset`. `AbsolutePosition` and `AbsoluteSize` stay in window
    --- pixels. 720 suits a layout drawn on a desktop.
    ReferenceHeight: number
    --- Whether this tree lays out inside `UIService.SafeAreaInsets` rather than against the whole
    --- window. On by default: a full-bleed background is the exception and a HUD clipped by a notch is
    --- the failure.
    ScreenInsets: boolean
    --- Whether this screen goes with the game to the next scene (ADR 0106) rather than with the scene
    --- it is in. A loading screen drawn before `SceneService:LoadScene` stays up while the scene
    --- changes under it, and a script takes it down on `SceneLoaded`.
    KeepOnSceneLoad: boolean
end

declare extern type BillboardGui extends Instance with
    --- Whether it is laid out and drawn at all.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- The part it hangs over: a part in the world, or a `Part2D` on the plane -- a name over a sprite,
    --- a health bar over a monster. Empty means its parent, when its parent is one of those; a
    --- billboard with neither is not drawn.
    Adornee: (BasePart | Part2D)?
    --- How big it is. **The scale is in metres and the offset in pixels**, and the two mean different
    --- things: a scale keeps its size in the world, so it shrinks with distance like anything else
    --- there; an offset keeps its size on the screen, so a name tag is as readable across the map as up
    --- close. The children lay out against the pixel size either way.
    Size: UDim2
    --- Where its centre sits relative to the adornee's centre, in metres and in the world's own axes --
    --- `vector.create(0, 3, 0)` is three metres above it however the part is turned.
    WorldOffset: vector
    --- Whether it shows through walls. Off, it is hidden by whatever is in front of it, like anything
    --- in the world.
    AlwaysOnTop: boolean
    --- How far from the camera it stops being drawn, in metres. Zero means no limit.
    MaxDistance: number
    --- How bright its colours are in the world's light, which is brighter than the screen's: above one,
    --- it glows and blooms.
    Brightness: number
end

declare extern type SurfaceGui extends Instance with
    --- Whether it is laid out and drawn at all.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- The part it covers. Empty means its parent, when its parent is a part.
    Adornee: BasePart?
    --- Which face of the part it covers. `Front` is the one the part's `LookVector` comes out of, and
    --- every side face is read the right way up.
    Face: EnumFace
    --- How many of the canvas's pixels fit in a metre of the face, which is what a child's offset and a
    --- `TextSize` are measured in. Fifty makes 14-point text about a hand high on a wall; more is finer
    --- text on the same face.
    PixelsPerMetre: number
    --- Whether it shows through what is in front of the face.
    AlwaysOnTop: boolean
    --- How bright its colours are in the world's light: above one, a screen that glows.
    Brightness: number
end

declare extern type UIObject extends Instance with
    --- Where this object's `AnchorPoint` sits inside its parent.
    Position: UDim2
    --- How big it is, relative to its parent plus an offset. Overridden on an axis that `AutomaticSize`
    --- covers.
    Size: UDim2
    --- Which point of THIS object `Position` places, in fractions of its own size. (0,0) is its
    --- top-left corner and (0.5,0.5) its middle -- which is what makes centring something one property
    --- rather than an arithmetic apology.
    AnchorPoint: Vector2
    --- Degrees clockwise about the anchor point. It rotates the DRAWING and not the layout: a rotated
    --- element still occupies its unrotated rectangle, which is what stops one spinning icon reflowing
    --- a list every frame. **Descendants turn with it**, and a descendant's own `Rotation` composes on
    --- top -- a dial rotated 30 degrees with a needle rotated 90 draws the needle at 120, about the
    --- dial's anchor and then its own. Three things the turn does not reach, all for the reason
    --- `UICorner` gives: layout, which never sees it; the hit test, which stays the unrotated
    --- rectangle, because a button you could see in one place and click in another is worse than one
    --- that does not turn; and `ClipsDescendants`, whose rectangle stays axis-aligned, so a turned
    --- child of a clipping parent is clipped by the parent's upright box.
    Rotation: number
    --- The fill behind the object's own content. No `3` suffix (§2.5, divergence #11).
    BackgroundColor: Color3
    --- 0 opaque, 1 invisible. A fully transparent background still lays out; whether it takes the
    --- pointer is `Active`'s to say.
    BackgroundTransparency: number
    --- Whether this object takes the pointer: whether a press lands on it, whether the pointer is over
    --- it, and whether a press there is the interface's -- the `uiConsumed` a game reads from
    --- `InputService.InputBegan` -- or goes on to the game and to whatever is under it. **Until you
    --- write it, it is what the object is.** A button, a `TextInput` and a `ScrollFrame` take the
    --- pointer. A `TextLabel` and an `ImageLabel` do not, so a press on the icon or the caption inside
    --- a button is a press on the button. A `Frame` takes it when it draws a background --
    --- `BackgroundTransparency` under 1 -- so a veil behind a dialog blocks what is under it, and a
    --- clear frame that only holds a layout lets every press through. Write it and what you wrote
    --- stands: `true` on a clear frame makes an invisible blocker, `false` on a button makes it
    --- something to look at. **Never while `InputService.PointerLocked`**: a locked pointer is the
    --- game's, and nothing takes it.
    Active: boolean
    --- Whether this object and its descendants are laid out, drawn and hit-tested. All three together:
    --- an invisible element that still swallowed clicks is the defect this sentence exists to rule out.
    Visible: boolean
    --- Draw order within this ScreenGui, highest last, ties broken by document order. It does not
    --- affect layout and does not nest: one flat ordering across the tree, because a per-parent
    --- stacking context is the part of CSS nobody can hold in their head.
    ZIndex: number
    --- Where this child sits in a `UIListLayout` whose `SortOrder` is `LayoutOrder`. Ignored otherwise.
    LayoutOrder: number
    --- Which axes size themselves to fit the contents, overriding that half of `Size`.
    AutomaticSize: EnumAutomaticSize
    --- Whether descendants are clipped to this object's rectangle. What a scrolling list needs and what
    --- a drop shadow must not have.
    ClipsDescendants: boolean
    --- Where the solver put this object's top-left corner, in window pixels. Read-only because it is an
    --- OUTPUT: writing it would be arguing with the layout rather than changing it. Valid after the
    --- layout that produced it, which is the frame the property was read in.
    read AbsolutePosition: Vector2
    --- The size the solver gave it, in window pixels.
    read AbsoluteSize: Vector2
    --- Whether a gamepad or the arrow keys can select this object (ADR 0128). **Until you write it, it
    --- is what the object is**: a button and a `TextInput` are, and nothing else is. The selected
    --- object is `UIService.SelectedObject`; the button that activates fires its `Activated`.
    Selectable: boolean
    --- What is selected when the selection moves up from this object. Nothing means the nearest
    --- selectable object above it, found from where they are.
    NextSelectionUp: UIObject?
    --- The same, moving down.
    NextSelectionDown: UIObject?
    --- The same, moving left.
    NextSelectionLeft: UIObject?
    --- The same, moving right.
    NextSelectionRight: UIObject?
    --- What is drawn over this object while it is selected, in place of the default outline: its
    --- background, picture, corners, gradient and strokes, at this object's box. Its children are not
    --- drawn.
    SelectionImageObject: UIObject?
    --- Fired when a pointer press and release both land on this object. Device-neutral by design
    --- (§2.5, divergence #17): a click, a tap and a bound gamepad button all produce it, which is why
    --- there is no `MouseButton1Click`.
    read Activated: Signal<()>
    --- Fired when the pointer moves onto this object. Replaces `MouseEnter`, and is named for a pointer
    --- rather than a mouse because a finger is one too.
    read PointerEntered: Signal<()>
    --- Fired when the pointer moves off it.
    read PointerExited: Signal<()>
    --- This object became `UIService.SelectedObject`.
    read SelectionGained: Signal<()>
    --- It stopped being it.
    read SelectionLost: Signal<()>
end

declare extern type Frame extends UIObject with
end

declare extern type ViewportFrame extends UIObject with
    --- A `Camera` inside the frame to look through. Nil frames what is inside automatically, from the
    --- front and a little above.
    CurrentCamera: Camera?
    --- The light from every direction.
    Ambient: Color3
    --- The colour of its one light.
    LightColor: Color3
    --- Which way its one light travels -- down and away from the viewer by default.
    LightDirection: vector
end

declare extern type TextLabel extends UIObject with
    --- What it says. UTF-8, and a codepoint the font has no glyph for draws the font's `.notdef` box
    --- rather than nothing: a label that silently drops characters is the failure mode that ships.
    Text: string
    --- No `3` suffix (§2.5, divergence #11).
    TextColor: Color3
    --- How see-through the text is: 0 solid, 1 not drawn at all. The text's own, apart from
    --- `BackgroundTransparency`, so a label can fade its words and keep its box, or the other way
    --- round. Values outside 0 to 1 are kept as written and drawn as the nearer end.
    TextTransparency: number
    --- The em size in pixels. An atlas is rasterized per font and integer size, so a fractional one is
    --- rounded for rasterization and the layout still uses what was asked for.
    TextSize: number
    --- The typeface, as an `asset://` URI to a TrueType file. Empty is the engine's own default face --
    --- **Inter**, OFL 1.1, vendored and staged beside the binary -- and so is the literal name `Inter`.
    --- A name that cannot be resolved falls back to the default and says so ONCE rather than once a
    --- frame: a label that vanished because its font name had a typo is a bug report about the label. A
    --- build whose content directory has no font at all falls back further, to a built-in vector face
    --- that is ASCII only, one weight and no kerning -- text still draws, which is the point of having
    --- a fallback. Glyphs are rasterised on demand and cached by face, size and codepoint, so a
    --- `TextSize` a tween is animating costs one entry per quarter-pixel rather than one per frame.
    Font: Content
    --- Where the text sits across the box: `Left`, `Center` or `Right`. Named for the text, because a
    --- layout's `HorizontalAlignment` places children and this places words; the values are the same
    --- enum a layout uses.
    TextXAlignment: EnumHorizontalAlignment
    --- Where the text sits down the box: `Top`, `Center` or `Bottom`.
    TextYAlignment: EnumVerticalAlignment
    --- Whether lines break at the box's width. Breaks at spaces, and mid-word only for a word wider
    --- than the box -- a word cut in half at a random letter is worse than one that overhangs.
    TextWrapped: boolean
    --- Whether `Text` is read as markup, so one label can change colour, size and weight part-way
    --- through (F3). The tags: `<b>` bold, `<i>` italic, `<u>` underlined, `<s>` struck through, `<font
    --- color="#rrggbb" size="20" transparency="0.5">` (any of the three, `color` also as `rgb(r, g,
    --- b)`), and `<br/>` for a line break. Tags nest and each closes its own. Write `&lt;`, `&gt;`,
    --- `&amp;`, `&quot;` and `&apos;` for the characters themselves. **A tag that is not understood is
    --- drawn as text**, so a typo shows as itself rather than vanishing. Bold is drawn from the label's
    --- one face rather than a bold file, and italic by leaning it. A `TextInput` shows its text plain
    --- whatever this says: its caret counts characters as typed, tags and all.
    RichText: boolean
    --- Whether `TextSize` is ignored and the text is rasterized at whatever size fills the box.
    --- Re-rasterized rather than scaled: there is no distance field in v1, and a stretched bitmap is
    --- what "scaled text" usually means and looks like.
    TextScaled: boolean
end

declare extern type TextButton extends TextLabel with
end

declare extern type TextInput extends TextLabel with
    --- Drawn in place of `Text` while it is empty and the field is unfocused.
    PlaceholderText: string
    --- The placeholder's colour.
    PlaceholderColor: Color3
    --- Several lines: Return makes a new one and Ctrl+Return submits, the text wraps at the field's
    --- width and scrolls to keep the caret in view, and Up and Down move between lines. A single-line
    --- field takes a pasted line break as a space.
    MultiLine: boolean
    --- False: read-only. It still takes focus, and its text can still be selected and copied -- a code
    --- or an address shown to be copied.
    Editable: boolean
    --- The most characters it holds; 0 is no limit. Characters as a reader sees them, so an accented
    --- letter or an emoji is one. What does not fit is kept out and `InputRejected` says what it was.
    MaxLength: number
    --- Drawn as `MaskCharacter`s: a password. Copying and cutting give nothing away; pasting still
    --- works. A phone raises its password keyboard.
    Masked: boolean
    --- What each character of a masked field is drawn as: its first character.
    MaskCharacter: string
    --- Focusing it empties it: a field that is typed afresh each time.
    ClearTextOnFocus: boolean
    --- Focusing it selects all of it, so typing replaces what is there.
    SelectAllOnFocus: boolean
    --- Return submits and lets go of the keyboard. False keeps it: a chat box, where the next message
    --- follows. `Submitted` fires either way.
    ReleaseFocusOnSubmit: boolean
    --- Escape puts back the text it had when it was focused, as it lets go.
    RevertOnEscape: boolean
    --- The on-screen keyboard a phone raises when it is focused.
    KeyboardType: EnumTextInputKeyboard
    --- Where the caret is: 1-based, in bytes, as a Luau string's positions are -- `#Text + 1` after the
    --- last character. -1 while it is not focused. Writing it moves the caret, snapped to a character.
    CursorPosition: number
    --- The other end of the selection, in the same positions as `CursorPosition`; -1 when nothing is
    --- selected. Writing it selects from there to the caret.
    SelectionStart: number
    --- Fired when this field starts receiving keystrokes. Focus is taken by a press and released by a
    --- press elsewhere; there is deliberately no settable `Focused` property beside this, because two
    --- fields could then both believe they had it.
    read Focused: Signal<()>
    --- Fired when it stops. `submitted` is true when the field was left by pressing Return rather than
    --- by clicking away -- the difference between "the player finished" and "the player went somewhere
    --- else"; `reason` says which of the four ways it was.
    read FocusLost: Signal<boolean, EnumFocusLossReason>
    --- Return was pressed, with the text as it was -- whether or not the field lets go
    --- (`ReleaseFocusOnSubmit`).
    read Submitted: Signal<string>
    --- The player changed the text -- typing, a paste, a cut, an undo -- with the text now. A script's
    --- own write to `Text` does not fire it; `GetPropertyChangedSignal("Text")` fires for both.
    read TextChanged: Signal<string>
    --- What `MaxLength` kept out of an edit.
    read InputRejected: Signal<string>
    --- Takes the keyboard, as a press on it would: another field loses it with
    --- `Enum.FocusLossReason.Script`. Takes effect at the next frame.
    function CaptureFocus(self): ()
    --- Lets go of the keyboard; `FocusLost` fires with `submitted` (false when omitted) and
    --- `Enum.FocusLossReason.Script`. Takes effect at the next frame.
    function ReleaseFocus(self, submitted: boolean?): ()
    --- Whether it has the keyboard now.
    function IsFocused(self): boolean
end

declare extern type ImageLabel extends UIObject with
    --- The picture, as an `asset://` URI. A compiled texture out of a pack or an ordinary PNG out of a
    --- source tree; both work, because dev mode is the mode people spend their time in. Empty draws
    --- nothing but still fills the background. A URI that names nothing, and a picture that has been
    --- asked for but has not finished loading, both draw as a flat `ImageColor` rectangle -- the same
    --- thing, because from a frame's point of view they are.
    Image: Content
    --- Multiplied into the image, so one white glyph sheet can be drawn in any colour. White leaves it
    --- alone.
    ImageColor: Color3
    --- How see-through the picture is: 0 solid, 1 not drawn at all. The picture's own, apart from
    --- `BackgroundTransparency`, as `TextTransparency` is a label's words' -- an icon fades and its box
    --- stays, or the other way round. Values outside 0 to 1 are kept as written and drawn as the nearer
    --- end.
    ImageTransparency: number
    --- How the picture fits the box. `Tile` is BOUNDED: past about four thousand tiles the picture
    --- stretches instead, because a one-pixel image tiled over a full-screen frame is nearly a million
    --- quads and the frame that built them would be the hitch. A visibly wrong picture is one somebody
    --- can find; a frame that stops responding is not.
    ScaleType: EnumScaleType
    --- For `ScaleType.Slice`, where the nine-slice cuts are, in SOURCE pixels. The four corners are
    --- drawn at their own size whatever the box is, which is how a panel keeps its rounded corners. A
    --- box narrower than its own two corners collapses the middle rather than drawing the corners on
    --- top of each other. An inverted or empty rectangle is kept as given rather than corrected: a
    --- slice quietly fixed up is a panel that draws wrong with no way to find out why.
    SliceCenter: Rect
    --- Where the part of the picture to show starts, in source pixels from its top-left corner: one
    --- frame of a sprite sheet, one icon of an atlas. Read with `ImageRectSize`.
    ImageRectOffset: Vector2
    --- How much of the picture to show, in source pixels from `ImageRectOffset`. Zero is the whole
    --- picture. **A negative size reads the rectangle backwards** -- mirrored across that axis -- which
    --- is what a mirror's picture needs (ADR 0107).
    ImageRectSize: Vector2
end

declare extern type ImageButton extends ImageLabel with
end

declare extern type ScrollFrame extends UIObject with
    --- How big the scrollable area is. Children lay out against this rather than against the frame's
    --- own size.
    CanvasSize: UDim2
    --- How far the canvas is scrolled, in pixels. Clamped to what the canvas allows on the next layout,
    --- so a write past the end settles at the end rather than showing emptiness.
    CanvasPosition: Vector2
    --- In pixels; zero draws no bar and still scrolls, which is what a touch surface wants. A bar
    --- appears only on an axis that can actually move: a canvas that fits its region gets none, because
    --- a bar for an axis with nowhere to go is a bar that lies. The thumb is as long a fraction of the
    --- track as the view is of the canvas, and never shorter than the bar is wide -- a thumb of two
    --- pixels in a very long canvas is a thumb nobody can grab. **The thumb can be dragged**: the
    --- canvas follows it, as far along the canvas as the thumb is along its track. The bar is drawn
    --- OUTSIDE the region's own clip, or it would scroll away with the content it reports on.
    ScrollBarThickness: number
    --- The bar's colour: the thumb in it, and the track under the thumb in it at a quarter of the
    --- thumb's opacity. A middle grey by default, which reads on a light panel and on a dark one.
    ScrollBarImageColor: Color3
    --- How see-through the bar is, from 0 (solid) to 1 (gone, while the frame still scrolls). Apart
    --- from `BackgroundTransparency`: a clear list keeps its bar.
    ScrollBarImageTransparency: number
    --- Whether a hand can scroll it: the wheel, a drag, a fling, the bar and a selection moved out of
    --- view. A script's `CanvasPosition` writes still move it -- a list that scrolls itself to the
    --- newest line can still be one nobody else scrolls.
    ScrollingEnabled: boolean
    --- Which axes a hand scrolls along. **A drag in a frame inside another goes to the one that scrolls
    --- the way the drag started**: a vertical list on a horizontal carousel scrolls down when the
    --- finger goes down and lets the carousel have a sideways drag, and the axis is kept until the
    --- finger lifts.
    ScrollingDirection: EnumScrollingDirection
    --- Whether a drag past the end gives -- less the further it goes -- and springs back when the
    --- finger lifts, and a fling that reaches the end bounces. `CanvasPosition` never leaves the
    --- canvas: the give is drawn, not reported.
    ElasticBehavior: EnumElasticBehavior
    --- Which axes the canvas grows along to hold its contents: a `UIListLayout`'s or a `UIGridLayout`'s
    --- line, or else the furthest any child reaches, with the frame's `UIPadding` around it. On such an
    --- axis the canvas is the larger of `CanvasSize` and the contents, so a list of rows added by a
    --- script scrolls without the script measuring them.
    AutomaticCanvasSize: EnumAutomaticSize
    --- How large the canvas is, in pixels, as the last layout found it -- never smaller than the frame,
    --- and with `AutomaticCanvasSize` taken into account.
    read AbsoluteCanvasSize: Vector2
    --- How much of the canvas shows at once, in pixels, as the last layout found it: the frame's own
    --- size. `AbsoluteCanvasSize - AbsoluteWindowSize` is how far it can scroll.
    read AbsoluteWindowSize: Vector2
end

